          <div class="table-wrapper">
            <table class="size-table" aria-label="Men EU to UK shoe size chart">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>EU Shoe Size</th>
                  <th>UK Shoe Size</th>
                  <th>Foot Length (CM)</th>
                  <th>Inches</th>
                  <th>US Men Size</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {menSizes.map(r => (
                  <tr>
                    <td class="primary-cell"><strong>EU {r.eu.toFixed(1)}</strong></td>
                    <td class="highlight-cell"><strong>UK {r.uk.toFixed(1)}</strong></td>
                    <td>{r.cm.toFixed(1)} cm</td>
                    <td>{r.inch.toFixed(1)}"</td>
                    <td>US {r.us.toFixed(1)}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Women's Table -->
        <div class="table-panel" id="tab-women" role="tabpanel">
          <h3 class="table-heading">Women's European to British Shoe Size Chart</h3>
          <div class="table-wrapper">
            <table class="size-table" aria-label="Women EU to UK shoe size chart">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>EU Shoe Size</th>
                  <th>UK Shoe Size</th>
                  <th>Foot Length (CM)</th>
                  <th>Inches</th>
                  <th>US Women Size</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {womenSizes.map(r => (
                  <tr>
                    <td class="primary-cell"><strong>EU {r.eu.toFixed(1)}</strong></td>
                    <td class="highlight-cell"><strong>UK {r.uk.toFixed(1)}</strong></td>
                    <td>{r.cm.toFixed(1)} cm</td>
                    <td>{r.inch.toFixed(1)}"</td>
                    <td>US {r.us.toFixed(1)}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Kids' Table -->
        <div class="table-panel" id="tab-kids" role="tabpanel">
          <h3 class="table-heading">Kids' European to British Shoe Size Chart</h3>
          <div class="table-wrapper">
            <table class="size-table" aria-label="Kids EU to UK shoe size chart">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>EU Kids Size</th>
                  <th>UK Kids Size</th>
                  <th>Foot Length (CM)</th>
                  <th>US Kids Size</th>
                  <th>Age Stage</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {kidsSizes.map(r => (
                  <tr>
                    <td class="primary-cell"><strong>EU {r.eu.toFixed(1)}</strong></td>
                    <td class="highlight-cell"><strong>UK {r.uk}</strong></td>
                    <td>{r.cm.toFixed(1)} cm</td>
                    <td>US {r.us}</td>
                    <td>{r.stage}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Editorial SEO Prose Section -->
    <article class="editorial-band">
      <div class="page-inner prose-article">
        <h2 class="editorial-main-title">How to Convert EU to UK Shoe Size: Complete Continental to British Guide</h2>
        
        <p class="lead-paragraph">
          If you are purchasing European fashion footwear from French, Italian, or German retailers, or ordering continental sneaker brands like Adidas, Puma, Veja, or Birkenstock to the United Kingdom, mastering your <strong>eu to uk shoe size</strong> conversion is essential. Because the two sizing conventions originate from different historical measurement standards, translating an <strong>eu size to uk</strong> requires understanding how European Paris Points relate to British barleycorns. This in-depth guide covers mathematical formulas, gender differences, reverse guidelines for <strong>uk size to eu shoes</strong>, and brand-specific fitting nuances.
        </p>

        <h3>The Measurement Difference: Paris Points vs. Barleycorns</h3>
        <p>
          Shoppers comparing <strong>equivalent shoe sizes uk europe</strong> often notice that European numbers run higher and increment more closely together than British numbers:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Continental Europe (Paris Point):</strong> The European system uses the Paris Point (<em>Point de Paris</em>), established in France in the 19th century. One Paris Point measures two-thirds of a centimeter (approximately 6.67 millimeters). European shoe sizes are unisex in length.</li>
          <li><strong>United Kingdom (Barleycorn):</strong> Codified under King Edward II, British shoe sizing increments by one barleycorn, which equals one-third of an inch (8.46 millimeters). Half sizes in the UK system advance by 1/6 of an inch (4.23 mm).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Because 6.67 mm is smaller than 8.46 mm, European sizes increase at a faster numerical rate than UK sizes. When translating <strong>european shoe size to uk</strong>, a single full step on the British scale (8.46 mm) covers roughly 1.27 steps on the European scale. Consulting our verified <strong>shoe size chart uk eu</strong> ensures you select the correct size across different footwear styles.
        </p>

        <h3>Core Formulas: Converting European Sizes to UK</h3>
        <p>
          To calculate your British footwear size when you know your continental <strong>european sizes to uk</strong>, follow these standard subtraction rules:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Men's Conversion (UK ≈ EU − 34):</strong> In adult men's footwear, subtracting 34 from the European size provides a reliable UK equivalent. For example, EU 42 − 34 = UK 8 (27.0 cm foot length); EU 43 − 34 = UK 9 (28.0 cm foot length); and EU 44 − 34 = UK 10 (29.0 cm foot length).</li>
          <li><strong>Women's Conversion (UK ≈ EU − 33):</strong> Because of slight differences in typical women's last allowances, subtracting 33 gives the standard UK women's size. For example, EU 38 − 33 = UK 5 (24.0 cm foot length); EU 39 − 33 = UK 6 (25.0 cm foot length); and EU 37 − 33 = UK 4 (23.5 cm foot length).</li>
          <li><strong>Popular Search Benchmark: UK Size 8 in EU:</strong> Shoppers frequently check <strong>uk size 8 in eu</strong>. A British men's size 8 maps directly to European <strong>EU 42.0</strong>, corresponding to an anatomical foot length of 27.0 centimeters (10.6 inches).</li>
          <li><strong>Popular Search Benchmark: UK Size 10 in EU:</strong> If you are looking up <strong>uk size 10 in eu</strong>, a British men's size 10 corresponds to <strong>EU 44.5 to EU 45.0</strong>, fitting an anatomical foot length of 29.0 centimeters (11.4 inches).</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>How to Convert Shoe Size EU to UK & Handle Half Sizes</h3>
        <p>
          When you <strong>convert shoe size eu to uk</strong> (or <strong>convert euro shoe size to uk</strong>), certain European sizes do not align perfectly with whole UK sizes:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>EU 40:</strong> Falls between UK 6.5 and UK 7.0. In sneakers and casual boots, UK 6.5 is common, whereas in formal dress shoes with tapered toes, UK 7.0 provides better forefoot comfort.</li>
          <li><strong>EU 41:</strong> Translates to UK 7.0 to 7.5 (fitting a 26.0 to 26.5 cm footbed).</li>
          <li><strong>EU 44:</strong> Corresponds to UK 9.5 to 10.0 (fitting a 28.5 to 29.0 cm footbed).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          If you have broader feet, higher insteps, or intend to wear heavy winter socks, choosing the larger half size when translating your <strong>eu foot size to uk</strong> prevents toe pinching and blisters.
        </p>

        <h3>Width Differences: British Welted Shoes vs. Continental Lasts</h3>
        <p>
          When comparing an <strong>euro foot size to uk</strong> or examining a <strong>uk foot size to eu</strong>, foot width plays a substantial role:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Northamptonshire British Lasts:</strong> Classic English shoemakers (such as Loake, Barker, Tricker’s, and Grenson) use standard letter widths: E (standard fit), F (medium-wide), and G (wide). English lasts tend to feature generous insteps and sturdy heel counters designed for double leather or Dainite rubber soles.</li>
          <li><strong>Italian and French Designer Shoes:</strong> Fashion footwear from Italy and France frequently utilizes sleeker, narrower lasts with pointed or chiseled toe boxes. If you have standard British F-width feet, sizing up half a size in Italian dress shoes is widely recommended.</li>
          <li><strong>German and Scandinavian Footwear:</strong> Brands like Birkenstock, Meindl, and Ecco prioritize natural orthopedic toe splay, providing wide toe boxes that align closely with comfortable UK fittings.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Measuring Your Foot at Home for an Exact Match</h3>
        <p>
          To eliminate fit uncertainty when ordering from European or British websites, take two minutes to measure your bare foot length:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Place a blank sheet of paper flat on a hard floor, with one edge touching a vertical wall.</li>
          <li>Stand straight with your heel lightly touching the wall, placing your full body weight on the foot.</li>
          <li>Use a pencil held perpendicular to the floor to mark the furthest tip of your longest toe.</li>
          <li>Measure the distance from the wall edge of the paper to the mark in centimeters.</li>
          <li>Look up your centimeter measurement in our <strong>shoe size chart uk eu</strong> above. For example, a 27.0 cm foot length corresponds to an EU 42 and a UK 8.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- FAQ Accordion Section -->
    <section class="faq-band">
      <div class="page-inner">
        <h2 class="section-title">Frequently Asked Questions: EU to UK Sizing</h2>
        <p class="section-sub">Expert answers to common questions about converting between European and British footwear sizes.</p>

        <div class="faq-accordion-list">
          {faqs.map((f, i) => (
            <details class="faq-item" open={i === 0}>
